<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Ibelin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ibelin family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ibelins were a noble family of the crusader states, named after the castle of Ibelin granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-of-ibelin">Barisan of Ibelin</a> around 1141. Through marriage they gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a>, and leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-the-old-lord-of-beirut">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</a>, dominated the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin.jpg" alt="House of Ibelin" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_maison_d%27Ibelin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
